  "summary": "Paithani (['pəɪ.ʈʰaːɳiː]) is a variety of sari, named after the Paithan in Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar district from the state of Maharashtra in India where the sari was first made by hand.",
  "description": "Paithani (['pəɪ.ʈʰaːɳiː]) is a variety of sari, named after the Paithan in Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar district from the state of Maharashtra in India where the sari was first made by hand. Present day Yeola, a town in Nashik, Maharashtra is the largest manufacturer of Paithani.\n\nPaithani is characterised by selvedges of an oblique square design, and a padar with a peacock design. Plain as well as spotted designs are available. Among other varieties, single coloured and kaleidoscope-coloured designs are also popular. The kaleidoscopic effect is achieved by using one colour for weaving lengthwise and another for weaving width wise.",
  "culturalContext": "Registered as a Geographical Indication of India (handicraft, Maharashtra; registration 2010–11), which protects the name for products made in its place of origin by traditional methods.",
